  "question": {
    "text": "In the circuit shown, a four-wire potentiometer is made of a <math><mn>400</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> long wire, which extends between <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> . The resistance per unit length of the potentiometer wire is <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>.</mo></math> If an ideal voltmeter is connected as shown with jockey <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi></math> at <math><mn>50</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> from end <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> , the expected reading of the voltmeter will be:<br /><img src=\"assets\\qt0275_367cd1f8efa3.png\" style=\"width: 380px; height: 216px;\" />"
